    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_business_and_management_processes_f:1739dbeef6" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_58">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">58</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description focuses on funding annual licenses and tokens for AI capabilities, without specific reference to acquisition workflows or historic nuclear data. The broader context lists the program interest area as “AI GENESIS PLATFORM,” suggesting enterprise AI tooling to support general business and management functions rather than a targeted acquisition system (AMIS) or digitization of historic nuclear records. This aligns best with the challenge centered on creating an integrated AI ecosystem and unified business/management platform to accelerate national security mission support processes.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">2500000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_business_and_management_processes_f:1739dbeef6" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_59">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">59</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description is broad—funding all labor and equipment for Artificial Intelligence investments—and the WBS name is simply “Artificial Intelligence - Program Work,” with STAT codes under Information Technology. The only additional clue is the program interest area “AI GENESIS PLATFORM,” which most closely aligns with the business and management–focused Genesis platform described in the “Accelerating Business and Management Processes for National Security Missions” challenge (unified business and management platform with integrated AI tools). There is no indication of historic nuclear data digitization or acquisition-specific tooling, so the business and management processes challenge is the best fit, though the generic wording limits confidence to medium.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">15148200.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:streamlining_production_removing_red_tape_and_en:5c22e0a6a3" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_57">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">57</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description focuses on “AI-enabled cybersecurity tools that support the enterprise,” and the STAT/WBS context is Enterprise Operations under Information Technology &amp; Cybersecurity. This aligns best with improving and securing enterprise-wide business/management platforms and workflows rather than directly targeting acquisition processes or production/safety in nuclear facilities. Among the options, the business and management challenge is the closest fit to enterprise IT and cybersecurity enablement for national security missions, though the evidence is somewhat general, so confidence is medium. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">467378982.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:streamlining_production_removing_red_tape_and_en:5c22e0a6a3" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_61">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">61</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope emphasizes enterprise connectivity, secure AI models and tools, data governance, cybersecurity, and AI workforce readiness as foundational capabilities for current operations and future Genesis AI initiatives. Among the challenges, this most closely aligns with the acquisition-focused mission, which explicitly calls for an AI platform integrated with enterprise acquisition systems, strong data governance, secure infrastructure, and auditable AI tools to improve operational efficiency across the nuclear enterprise. The funding appears to underwrite broad AI platform and infrastructure capabilities that would directly enable the Accelerating Nuclear Enterprise Acquisition challenge and related acquisition operations, even though the description is general rather than acquisition-specific, hence medium confidence. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">636625000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_60">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">60</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope describes a mixed-classification high-performance computing resource to support NNSA’s growing mission demand, with emphasis on efficient use of HPC across classifications. Among the options, this aligns best with the nuclear threat assessment challenge, which explicitly calls out leveraging NNSA’s HPC resources and cross-classification data governance for mission-critical, physics-informed AI in nuclear emergency and threat-response contexts. The other two challenges focus on business/management workflows or design–production integration for deterrence rather than a generalized mixed-classification HPC platform. Because the description is generic and references an NA-114 whitepaper for details, confidence is medium rather than high. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">45000000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
